# THE TWO PACKAGES THIS REPOSITORY ACTUALLY ASKS FOR. Everything else in
# `dns/requirements.txt` is their transitive tree, and that file is COMPILED FROM THIS ONE —
# do not hand-edit it, and in particular never hand-write a hash line:
#
#     uv pip compile --generate-hashes --universal --python-version 3.13 \
#         dns/requirements.in -o dns/requirements.txt
#
# PINNED EXACTLY, NOT FLOATED, because the safety argument in `config.yaml` is an argument
# about these versions' BEHAVIOUR — that a reject list refuses to touch a record rather than
# deleting it, and that `pagerules` stays off so the www redirect is invisible rather than
# removed. `test_filters.py` re-proves that behaviour on whatever is actually installed, so a
# bump that breaks it goes red in `dns.yml` rather than in the zone.
#
# THE COMPILED FILE CARRIES A HASH FOR EVERY DISTRIBUTION, and that is a different claim from
# this one. Two exact pins leave eleven transitive packages resolving to whatever PyPI serves
# that minute, in the `apply` job — the one process in this repository where third-party code
# shares an interpreter with a write credential to the domain's DNS, in a repository that
# SHA-pins every GitHub Action for exactly that threat. `--require-hashes` on the install is
# what turns a missing hash into a failed job instead of a silent download.
#
# `--python-version 3.13` IS `dns.yml`'s `PYTHON_VERSION` AND MUST STAY THAT WAY. It is the
# floor a universal resolution is computed against; `tests/dns-config.test.ts` holds the two
# together, because a lock resolved for an interpreter the workflow no longer runs is a lock
# proving nothing about the install that happens.
